course: dictionary
lesson: 14994
title: Strong words for saying no
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Repudiate is a strong, formal word. It means to say firmly that something is wrong or untrue, or to refuse to accept or support it. Look at the clues: «The leader stood up and repudiated the rumour, saying it was a lie.» Saying it was a lie tells you that repudiate means firmly rejecting something as false.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping in a simpler word. «The leader rejected the rumour» still makes sense, so you are on the right track. Repudiate is a stronger word than just saying no, because it shows the person is very sure.
explain: In law and history, repudiate means to refuse to keep an agreement or to refuse to pay a debt. Read this: «The company tried to repudiate the contract and would not pay the money it owed.» The words would not pay are the clue that the meaning is about refusing a deal.
explain: Old books sometimes use repudiate to mean something personal: saying you will have nothing more to do with someone. «She repudiated her brother after the quarrel and never spoke to him again.» The words never spoke to him again are the clue. This meaning is old-fashioned, so it is rare today.
fact: Repudiate is a verb: repudiates, repudiated, repudiating.
fact: Repudiate means to refuse to accept or support something, or to say firmly that it is wrong or not true.
fact: In law, repudiate means to refuse to keep an agreement or to refuse to pay a debt.
fact: Old-fashioned use: to say you will have nothing more to do with someone.
fact: You will often meet repudiate in phrases such as «repudiate a claim», «repudiate the treaty» and «firmly repudiated».
exercise: «The leader repudiated the rumour and said it was a lie.» What does repudiated mean? | Repeated word for word | Forgotten completely | Spread and shared widely | *Firmly rejected as untrue
exercise: «The company tried to repudiate the contract and would not pay.» What does repudiate mean here? | Sign a new contract with joy | Lend money to a friend | Read the contract aloud to friends | *Refuse to keep an agreement
exercise: «The judge firmly repudiated the claim.» Find and copy the word that means the same as repudiated. | *rejected | judge | firmly | claim
test: «The king repudiated the treaty, so the countries were at war again.» What does repudiated suggest? | He signed the treaty with a smile | He lost the treaty in the post | *He refused to accept the treaty | He read the treaty aloud to everyone
test: Lily said, «I repudiate you. I never want to speak to you again.» What does repudiate mean here? | Hug someone tightly | Ask for help | Ask for a new name | *Say you will have nothing more to do with someone
exam: Which word is closest in meaning to repudiate in «The witness firmly repudiated the accusation»? | Accept | Celebrate | *Reject | Forget
